[{:seon.task/name "a-cold-node-decodes-in-one-pass"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-10: the first read of a stored index node costs about 610-750 ticks and 2 KB a datom (69 M of a 94 M-tick scan of 113k ``:form/refs`` datoms was cold decode; the second scan 9.7 M); one konserve read of a small branch head costs 11.7k ticks and 33 KB; the fasl decode of the 17 MB reach graph 37 M ticks. It is the floor under a root's boot (`seon.db/preload!` is 1.2 s of every boot and is decode work), every law that reads the program, the first scan in any test process, and each open. Should cost: at most about 100 ticks and 250 B a datom: its share of one zstd frame, five fields read, one object built (a four-field deftype is 8 ticks and 128 B on the Jolt at upstream's head). Suspected by reading, not measured: a node is decoded into generic vectors and the datoms are built from those afterwards; the leaf's array is grown, not made at its known length; keywords are interned again for each datom. First step: one counted measure of a cold scan split into decompress, fasl read and datom construction, which names the largest; no design before that number. Where: `konserve.jolt.fasl-store`, the node read handlers in `datahike.index.persistent-set`, the sorted set's `restore-node`. Proof when built: a cold scan of n datoms costs a stated number per datom, at sizes to 1,000."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.index.persistent-set-test/a-cold-node-decodes-in-one-pass}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-collections-mark-costs-what-changed", :seon.task/plan "After the one-read mark (deps, 2026-11-14), one boot's mark of the standing store (64 branches, the cutoff of seon.db/retain!) still reads 328,440 nodes raw (11.5 GB, 2.1B ticks, 28 s wall) to find 9,152 keys to sweep: it reads every reachable node at every collection, though a node never changes at its address, so the out-of-line values it references and its children stay what the last collection found. Design with runtime, the collection's caller: a mark proportional to what changed (the nodes written since the last collection, and the commits since its cutoff), the found references kept with the store so a node read once is not read again; and whether a collection's reads may run in root's process at all, beside its working set. Also: konserve's read-immutable copies the value bytes three times (~6 KB of a read's ~35 KB). Done: a boot's mark reads the nodes written since the last one, measured against these numbers. docs/storage.md#collection.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-collection-reads-only-the-nodes-written-since-the-last}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-leaf-decodes-only-the-values-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. Found 2026-11-16 (deps, root's order: the judge's plan memory, every seon_done dying at the heap ceiling). MEASURED [deps' agent]: a cold EAVT seek on the results branch costs 177k ticks and 889 KB, warm 2k ticks and 3 KB (result/ae4fe7ad); verdicts-at over 2,300 digests costs 1.8B ticks and 8.5 GB on each of three runs in a row, nothing kept (result/ce93792c). CAUSE [read]: the store writes a value of at least :seon.config.store/value-out-of-line-bytes (1,024) under its own key and a ValueRef in the leaf (out-of-line), but restore's in-line (persistent_set.cljc, CachedStorage restore) fetches and decodes EVERY ValueRef of a leaf it reads, so a seek of one small attribute decodes every neighbour's large value: on the results branch each result's :seon.proof/reads (mean 13 KB EDN, 1,208 keys in the sample; 44,149 of them, result/ada0b0a4), and the node cache (128 MB, node-cache/node-bytes weighing the restored values) evicts as fast as it loads. Same cost in seon.vector/near's cold :seon.vector/f16 reads (task seon.vector, its H). FIX (the library's, AGENTS 3a.5: a named read costs only what it names): a live leaf keeps its ValueRefs; a datom's value is fetched when a datom leaves the index (slice, seek, rslice) or a comparison reaches its v (same e and a), its key's value kept once in the node cache by content key; a rewritten leaf keeps a ValueRef as it is, never fetching it. To settle first: every reader of a leaf's keys inside Datahike (comparators, datoms-diff, transact's lookups, gc's refs) and Datom equality, which must never see a ValueRef against a plain value. Off the judge's path once the results model stops carrying read sets as one value per result (tests2's, the reads as datoms on the newest green); this task is for every other large value. ESTIMATE: a cold seek decodes the leaf's small datoms and fetches only the values it returns: ~10k ticks and ~20 KB for a leaf of ~64 datoms, against 177k / 889 KB; a value read is one konserve get and decode (~13 KB: ~30k ticks). Proof, red first: datahike.test.out-of-line-test (a seek of one small attribute in a leaf whose neighbours hold large out-of-line values fetches none of them)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.test.out-of-line-test/a-seek-fetches-only-the-values-it-returns}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
]
